# Plan: <title>

> Written by `/implement` **after** the plan was approved and **before** the first code edit.
> Living until the PR merges: if the implementation departs from this, update it in the same
> commit. `/review` checks the diff against the *Files that change* section.

## Source

What this implements and where the design lives (`ADR-NNN` §…, `SPEC-NNN §R<n>`, issue `#N`).
One paragraph; link, do not restate.

## Files that change

Exhaustive. A file edited that is not listed here is review-visible drift.

| File | Change | Layer |
|---|---|---|
| `path/to/file` | new / modified / deleted — one line on what | domain / application / infrastructure / presentation / contract / test / docs |

## Order of work

1. …
2. …

Call out the step that is riskiest and why.

## Not changing

Scope boundary — what a reviewer might expect to see changed and will not, and why.

## What could break

Answer the interrogation question *"what could this change break?"* — the callers, tests and
contracts in the blast radius (`/impact-check` output), and the behaviour a user would notice if
one of them did. If the answer is "nothing", say why (leaf module, additive-only, feature-flagged).

## Risks

Known hazards (hub modules, shared schemas, migrations, rate limits, concurrency) and the
mitigation for each. Name the **single most risky step** from *Order of work* and what makes it so.

## Alternatives not chosen

The options considered and rejected, one line each with the reason. A reviewer who would have
chosen differently needs to see that the option was weighed, not missed.

## Proof

What demonstrates the change works — the specific tests that must pass, the screenshot or
browser flow for UI, the contract diff. `/pr` pastes this evidence into the PR body.

## Departures from plan

Appended during implementation, one line each: what changed vs. the plan above and why.
Empty means the plan was followed as written.
